course: technology
lesson: 18
title: Car safety: brakes, seat belts and airbags
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: A moving car is heavy and fast, and it cannot simply stop: the brakes have to turn all that movement into heat, which takes time and road.
explain: If a car does stop suddenly in a crash, everything inside that is not held keeps going forward at the same speed, including people, which is why seat belts matter on every single trip.
explain: Modern cars are built to protect the people inside: the belt holds you, the airbag cushions the head, and the front of the car crumples on purpose to slow everyone more gently.
explain: Picture the school run: you climb into your booster seat, click the belt across your chest and hips, and later hop out on the pavement side, away from the traffic and the cyclists.
explain: Outside a car the rules matter just as much: drivers cannot see everything, so you never play behind parked cars and you keep clear of anything reversing.
fact: Pressing the brake pedal pushes fluid along pipes that squeeze pads onto discs on the wheels, and the rubbing turns the car's movement into heat.
fact: A car cannot stop instantly; even at 30 miles an hour it needs several car-lengths, and more on a wet road.
fact: ABS anti-lock brakes notice when a wheel stops turning and skids, and release and press the brakes many times a second so the driver can still steer.
fact: A seat belt locks if the car suddenly slows or the belt is pulled fast, and it is worn on every trip in every seat, across the chest and hips, not the neck or tummy.
fact: The three-point seat belt was invented at Volvo in 1959, and the company let other carmakers use it for free.
fact: In a crash, sensors set off a small charge that fills a nylon airbag with gas in less time than a blink, bursting out with great force, and then it quickly empties.
fact: Children sit in the back, and a rear-facing baby seat never goes in front of an active airbag.
fact: A child seat or booster raises a child so the belt crosses the strong bones of the chest and hips; in the UK children use one until 12 years old or 135 cm tall, whichever comes first.
fact: Babies ride facing backwards because their necks are weak.
fact: Child locks are small switches on the edge of the back doors that stop them opening from inside, and passengers get out on the pavement side and check for bikes; opening a door into a cyclist is called dooring.
fact: Drivers have blind spots behind and to the sides, and a small child can be hidden behind a car; lorries have huge blind spots, and if you cannot see the driver's mirrors, the driver cannot see you.
fact: Parking sensors send out sound pulses and time the echo, beeping faster as the car gets closer to something, a bit like a bat, but they do not see everything.
fact: Speed cameras measure speed with radar or by timing a car between two points; a person hit at 20 mph is much less likely to be killed than at 30 mph, which is why there are 20 mph zones near schools.
fact: The front and back of a car are crumple zones, built to squash in a crash and take up energy so the people inside are slowed more gently.
exercise: How do a car's brakes slow it down? | Pads squeeze discs and the rubbing turns movement into heat | A parachute opens behind the car | The engine is switched off and the car rests | The tyres let out their air
exercise: Why can't a car stop straight away? | Its movement has to be turned into heat, which takes distance | Brakes only work after ten seconds | The driver has to switch off the radio first | Cars have no way to slow down at all
exercise: Why does skidding make it hard to steer? | A sliding wheel can't grip the road to turn the car | The steering wheel locks tight as soon as you brake | Skidding switches off the engine | The tyres go flat in a skid
exercise: What do ABS anti-lock brakes do? | Release and press the brakes many times a second so the driver can steer | Make the car stop in one metre | Lock all four wheels at once | Switch the brakes off on wet roads
exercise: Why does a seat belt lock when you pull it fast? | It locks on a sudden pull or stop to hold you in place | It has run out of belt | The car wants you to sit still | It only locks when the engine is off and the doors are all shut
exercise: Where should the seat belt cross your body? | Across the chest and hips | Across the neck and the soft tummy | Under both arms, like a backpack | Behind your back, so it doesn't itch
exercise: Who invented the three-point seat belt and shared it for free? | Volvo, in 1959 | Henry Ford, in 1908 | A racing team, in 2000 | The police, in 1850
exercise: How fast does an airbag fill? | In less time than a blink | In about ten seconds | In about a minute | It never fills, it is always full
exercise: Why can airbags hurt small children in the front? | They burst out with great force and speed | They are filled with very hot water | They squash the child down for a long, long time | They are made of sharp metal
exercise: Why do younger children need a booster seat? | So the belt crosses the strong chest and hip bones | So they can see out of the window | So they can reach the radio | So the car is balanced better on corners and doesn't tip over
exercise: In the UK, until when do children use a child seat? | Until 12 years old or 135 cm tall, whichever comes first | Until they are 5 years old | Until they can tie their shoes and do up the belt all by themselves | Until they are 180 cm tall
exercise: Why do babies ride facing backwards? | Their necks are weak | They like to see the back window | It stops them crying on long trips | It keeps them away from the sun
exercise: Why do you get out of a car on the pavement side? | Away from traffic and cyclists who might hit the door | Because the other door is locked forever | To be closer to the shops | To keep your shoes dry and out of the puddles in the gutter by the road
exercise: Why can't a lorry driver see you right next to the cab? | Lorries have huge blind spots | Lorry drivers wear dark glasses | The windows are painted over | Lorries have no mirrors at all
exercise: How does a car's parking sensor know how close the wall is? | It sends sound pulses and times the echo | It feels the wall with a little stick | It smells the bricks as it gets close | It asks the driver by beeping once at the start
exercise: Why are there 20 mph zones near schools? | A person hit at 20 mph is much less likely to be killed than at 30 | Cars are not allowed to go faster than buses | Children walk at 20 mph | Speed cameras only work at 20
exercise: Why are cars made to crumple at the front and back? | Squashing takes up energy so people inside are slowed more gently | So cars are cheaper to make | So they can fit in small parking spaces | So the bonnet opens easily
exercise: Mum, Dad and 3 kids go in the car. How many seat belts need clicking? | 5 | 3 | 4 | 6
test: ugh do I need the belt, it's only to the shop round the corner? | Yes, every trip, in every seat | No, only on long motorway trips | No, only grown-ups need one in a car | Only if it's raining and the road is wet
test: My brother tucks the belt under his arm cos it's itchy on his neck. What's right? | He needs a booster so the belt sits on his chest and hips | That is the safest way to wear it | It doesn't matter where it goes | He should put it round his soft tummy instead, under his jumper, so it doesn't rub
test: What would happen to an unbelted passenger if the car stopped suddenly? | They would keep going forward and hit the inside of the car | They would stop at exactly the same moment as the car, safe in the seat | They would be pushed back into the seat | Nothing, cars stop gently
test: A baby seat facing backwards is put in the front seat with the airbag on. Is it safe? | No, a rear-facing baby seat never goes in front of an active airbag | Yes, the airbag will protect the baby | Yes, the front seat is the safest | No, babies must face forwards
test: In a cartoon a car stops dead from full speed in a second at a puddle. What's wrong? | Cars need several car-lengths to stop, longer when wet | Nothing, cars stop instantly in water | Puddles make brakes stronger | Wet roads make cars stop sooner, because the water acts like glue on the tyres
test: Ted's playing hide and seek behind a parked car. What's the rule? | Never play behind or near parked cars | It's fine if the car is small | It's fine if the engine is off for a minute | It's fine if you crouch down low
test: mate says a crumpled car after a crash means it was badly made. true? | Not necessarily; crumpling often means it did its job | Yes, good cars never dent | Yes, crumpling hurts the people inside more than a stiff car would | No, crumpled cars are always made of paper
test: The parking sensor isn't beeping, so there's no one behind the car. Right? | No, sensors don't see everything; still stay clear | Yes, the sensor never misses anything, even a small child or a cat | Yes, if the screen is dark | No, the sensor only sees walls
test: You're on your bike next to a big lorry and you can't see its mirrors. What does that mean? | The driver can't see you either | The lorry is parked for good | The mirrors are folded for cleaning | The driver is waving at you
test: Which is true about an airbag after it fills? | It quickly empties again | It stays full for an hour | It fills the whole car up | It can be used again at once
test: What would ABS do if the driver slammed the brakes on ice? | Stop the wheels locking so the driver can still steer | Make the car go faster | Turn off the brakes completely so the tyres can slide along the ice | Spin the car round on purpose
exam: Identify the principal function of a crumple zone in a motor vehicle. | The absorption of energy so that occupants are decelerated more gently | The reduction of the cost of manufacture | The provision of additional storage space | The improvement of the vehicle's appearance
exam: It is given that a child of nine years measures 130 centimetres. Ascertain, pursuant to United Kingdom rules, whether a child seat is required. | A child seat or booster is required | No child seat is required | A child seat is required only on motorways | The child must sit in the front seat
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the operation of anti-lock braking systems? | The brakes are released and reapplied many times per second to retain steering | The wheels are locked so as to shorten all stopping distances | The brakes are disabled upon wet surfaces | The engine is reversed to arrest the vehicle
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the placement of a rear-facing infant seat. | It must never be placed before an active front airbag | It must always be placed in the front seat beside the driver | It must face forwards in all circumstances | It requires no seat belt whatsoever
